   "subject": "accounting for topography; plant distance sampling",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "My colleagues and I have recently used line transect distance sampling to=\r<br> estimate density of flowering stems of a tall, very brightly-flowered fo=\r<br>rb in grassland.  The plant is visible with the naked eye up to 150 m awa=\r<br>y. However, hills often obstruct our view (For instance, a small ridge wi=\r<br>th its crest 15 m east of the observer will restrict detection distance t=\r<br>o 15 m in that direction, whereas we may be able to view these plants 150=\r<br> m to the west). I would like suggestions on how to best analyze data fro=\r<br>m these surveys.\r<br>\r<br> I have used MCDS to account for variation in detectability due to habita=\r<br>t; for instance, using grassland vegetation height as a covariate, with f=\r<br>ocal organisms in areas with taller grasses being slightly more difficult=\r<br> to see than in areas with shorter grasses.  But in our current study, as=\r<br> we walk a transect, the existence of small hills to one side or the othe=\r<br>r make detection beyond the hill impossible (unless we create and walk mo=\r<br>re transects, which is an option). But even if we create more transects, =\r<br>how should we analyze the distance data from each transect, when maximum =\r<br>detection distance can change so greatly along the transect due to hills?=\r<br>\r<br>\r<br>If you like, please email responses to me:\r<br>\r<br><a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mor...@iastate.edu</a>\r<br>\r<br>I would be happy to compile the responses I receive and summarize them to=\r<br> the list.\r<br>\r<br>-Ray Moranz\r<br>\r<br>--\r<br>distance-sampling list home page:\r<br>  <a href=\"http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792477612903000&amp;usg=AOvVaw19w35a34F04y719WQtUpPW\">http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/lists/distance-sampling.html</a>\r<br>To unsubscribe, go to the home page and click &quot;Join or leave the list&quot;,\r<br>  or email <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">jisc...@jiscmail.ac.uk</a> with the following message body:\r<br>  leave distance-sampling\r<br>Before posting to the list, read the list guidelines at\r<br>  <a href=\"http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792477612903000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Ysa-lizj4_DBcZXH82AIf\">http://www.jiscmail.ac.uk/files/distance-sampling/</a>\r<br>    distance-sampling.welcome\r<br>Any questions, email the list owner at\r<br>  <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">distance-sam...@jiscmail.ac.uk</a>\r<br>--\r<br>"
    ]
